<section class="case-sec" id="problem">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§A — One feature set, built once</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Every ML effort in Azure Storage — customer similarity, anomaly
        detection, forecasting, workload segmentation, LLM-based reasoning for
        the customer-health agent — was rebuilding its own feature set from the same raw
        telemetry. There was no shared representation of what an "account"
        actually looked like at scale.
      </p>
      <p>
        This is the shape of wasted ML work inside big orgs: every team ends up
        with its own slightly-different version of the same derived features,
        their models can't be compared, their anomaly scores can't be joined,
        and the next team starts over. Account2Vec's premise: if ~100 raw
        metrics describe an account, there's a much smaller latent space that
        captures the meaningful behavioral variation. <strong>Learn that space once,
        hand the vectors out, let downstream teams reason in shared
        coordinates.</strong>
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="architecture">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§B — Pipeline shape</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Four stages: raw storage-service telemetry aggregates into the
        <strong>AccountFeatureMatrix</strong> — a stable, versioned view of ~40–50 rolling
        per-account features; the autoencoder compresses those into a dense
        embedding; embeddings pool hierarchically into subscription and
        customer tiers; and the whole surface is instrumented with drift,
        coverage, and staleness monitoring.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% include "partials/diagram-a2v.njk" %}
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      The design decision that mattered most was <strong>separating the feature
      matrix from the model</strong>. The matrix is the platform contract — other
      teams can consume it directly even without embeddings. The embeddings are
      an optional-but-powerful second layer. The model is the least interesting
      part, which is precisely the argument in the
      <a href="/#judgment">judgment calls</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="schema">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§C — The AccountFeatureMatrix schema</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The single most important artifact in the system. If this schema isn't
        stable, nothing downstream can compound — so it's versioned,
        column-level documented, and treated as a product contract.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="table-panel panel reveal">
