<section class="case-sec" id="training">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§D — The training loop</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A standard denoising autoencoder — the interesting engineering is
        everything around it: batch composition, scheduling, checkpointing,
        and the hierarchical pool.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="code-panel panel reveal">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>// a2v/model.py</span><span class="g">representative · abridged</span></div>
    <pre><code><span class="kw">class</span> <span class="hl">AccountEncoder</span>(nn.Module):
    <span class="kw">def</span> __init__(self, n_features=52, d=32):
        super().__init__()
        self.enc = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Linear(n_features, 128), nn.GELU(), nn.Dropout(0.1),
            nn.Linear(128, 64), nn.GELU(),
            nn.Linear(64, d),          <span class="cm"># bottleneck = embedding</span>
        )
        self.dec = nn.Sequential(
            nn.Linear(d, 64), nn.GELU(),
            nn.Linear(64, 128), nn.GELU(), nn.Dropout(0.1),
            nn.Linear(128, n_features),
        )

    <span class="kw">def</span> forward(self, x, mask):
        z = self.enc(x * mask)         <span class="cm"># null-mask raw inputs</span>
        xr = self.dec(z)
        <span class="kw">return</span> z, xr

<span class="cm"># training step (abridged)</span>
<span class="kw">for</span> batch <span class="kw">in</span> loader:                  <span class="cm"># stratified by region + age</span>
    x, mask = batch["feats"], batch["mask"]
    x_noisy = x + noise(x, p=0.15)     <span class="cm"># denoising</span>
    z, xr = model(x_noisy, mask)
    loss = masked_mse(xr, x, mask) + \
           0.01 * z.pow(2).mean()      <span class="cm"># soft unit-norm</span>
    loss.backward(); opt.step(); opt.zero_grad()</code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      Two details matter: <strong>null-masking the loss</strong>, so brand-new accounts with
      missing history don't pull the embedding toward zero, and a <strong>soft
      unit-norm penalty</strong> on the bottleneck, so cosine similarity is meaningful
      out of the box — which is what downstream teams actually want.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="downstream">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§E — The payoff query</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        Once embeddings exist, any team can ask "find me 20 accounts similar to
        this one" without writing a model.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="code-panel panel reveal">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>// downstream: similarity search</span><span class="g">cosine over 32-d embeddings</span></div>
    <pre><code><span class="cm"># seed: a newly-escalated account</span>
seed = a2v.get(account_id="stacc-7f1a…")        <span class="cm"># (32,)</span>

<span class="cm"># k-NN over the fleet index — millions of accounts</span>
neighbors = a2v.knn(
    seed, k=20,
    filters=dict(region="apac", capacity_tb=(5, 50), age_months=(3, 36)),
)

<span class="kw">for</span> n <span class="kw">in</span> neighbors:
    print(n.account_id, n.cosine, n.top_drivers)

<span class="cm"># → nearest accounts share workload shape even if sized differently</span>
<span class="cm"># → used by the customer-health agent to surface "similar at-risk customers"</span>
<span class="cm"># → used by forecasting to borrow seasonality from peers</span>
<span class="cm"># → used by anomaly to calibrate expected ranges per cohort</span></code></pre>
  </div>
  <div class="prose reveal">
    <p>
      Downstream teams don't always reason at the account level, so the platform
      exposes pooled embeddings at two higher tiers — <strong>account → subscription →
      customer</strong>. The pooling is a weighted mean with per-axis attention
      learned against a small set of aggregation tasks, not a plain average:
      naive averaging collapses bimodal customers — two very different
      subscriptions — into a meaningless middle point.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="siblings">
  <div class="reveal">
    <div class="sec-mark">§F — The guardrail sibling</div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A representation other teams are meant to depend on needs to know it's
        wrong before they do. The drift, coverage, and staleness plumbing here is
        the same discipline that shipped separately as the
        <a href="/case/data-trust/">data-trust platform</a> — freshness, volume
        and schema checks with quality gates, hourly profiling of
        <strong>400+ production datasets</strong>, and the lineage engine that
        maps where any of it actually came from.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

